ng."}}},{"type":"PullRequestReviewCommentEvent","created_at":"2026-09-14T19:51:14Z","repo":{"name":"scrapy/scrapy"},"payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"body":"I'll wait for your reply here, but I'm leaning to the error approach as I think it's makes the API more explicit than swallowing the error - what do you think?"}}},{"type":"PullRequestReviewCommentEvent","created_at":"2026-09-14T19:50:28Z","repo":{"name":"scrapy/scrapy"},"payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"body":"Updated. Please let me know if it's ok for you. Thanks."}}},{"type":"PullRequestReviewCommentEvent","created_at":"2026-09-14T19:43:30Z","repo":{"name":"scrapy/scrapy"},"payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"body":"Agree that this is not necessary. Although we could have more test cases it doesnt contribute. I'll remove the parametrization here."}}},{"type":"PullRequestReviewCommentEvent","created_at":"2026-09-14T19:42:40Z","repo":{"name":"scrapy/scrapy"},"payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"body":"Hi! Thanks for the comment.\r\n\r\nMy intent here was to avoid exposing the unpacking error and to follow the behavior proposed in [#8131](https://github.com/scrapy/scrapy/issues/8131), treating -u user like -u user:. However, I agree that this only approximates curl’s interactive prompt. If preserving that distinction is preferable, I can instead raise a descriptive ValueError for a missing password.\r\n\r\nDo you think that this would be preferable? if so, please let me know and I'll make the change."}}},{"type":"PullRequestEvent","created_at":"2026-09-13T14:15:54Z","repo":{"name":"scrapy/scrapy"},"payload":{"action":"opened"}}]}
